{"name":"Navajo Turquoise","hex":"#007c78","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"turquoise","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A sky-blue, greenish-blue, or greenish-gray semi-precious gemstone.","A pale greenish-blue colour, like that of the gemstone."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Made of turquoise (the gemstone).","Having a pale greenish-blue colour."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/turquoise"}],"entries":[{"title":"Navajo","blocks":[{"text":"The Navajo are an Indigenous People of the Southwestern United States. The Navajo term for themselves is Diné. Their language is Navajo, a Southern Athabaskan language. The states with the largest Diné populations are Arizona (140,263) and New Mexico (108,305). More than three-quarters of the Diné population resides in these two states."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/ff/Manuelito.jpg/330px-Manuelito.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Navajo","wikidata":"Q108266"},{"title":"Turquoise","blocks":[{"text":"Turquoise is an opaque, blue-to-green mineral that is a hydrous phosphate of copper and aluminium, with the chemical formula CuAl6(PO4)4(OH)8·4H2O. It is rare and valuable in finer grades and has been prized as a gemstone for millennia due to its hue."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7d/Turquoise-40031.jpg/330px-Turquoise-40031.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Turquoise","wikidata":"Q165254"},{"title":"Turquoise (color)","blocks":[{"text":"Turquoise is a cyan color, based on the mineral of the same name."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Turquoise_(color)","wikidata":"Q5960345"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2b/19th_century_knowledge_indian_lore_navaho_house.jpg/500px-19th_century_knowledge_indian_lore_n